Your tests are a textbook case for **parameterized tests**. Every method follows the same pattern: create validator → call `Validate(input)` → assert `IsValid` + `ErrorMessage`. Use MSTest's `[DataRow]` to collapse them:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class InputValidatorTests
{
    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("",        false, "Input cannot be empty")]
    [DataRow(null,      false, "Input cannot be empty")]
    [DataRow("   ",     false, "Input cannot be empty")]
    [DataRow("ab",      false, "Input must be at least 3 characters")]
    [DataRow("hello<script>", false, "Input contains invalid characters")]
    [DataRow("hello-world",   true,  null)]
    [DataRow("abc",           true,  null)]
    public void Validate_ReturnsExpectedResult(
        string? input, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
    {
        var result = new InputValidator().Validate(input);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }
}
```

For the length-based cases (`new string(...)`) that can't be `DataRow` constants, use `[DynamicData]`:

```csharp
public static IEnumerable<object[]> LengthCases =>
    new[]
    {
        new object[] { new string('x', 256), false, "Input must not exceed 255 characters" },
        new object[] { new string('a', 255), true,  null! },
    };

[DataTestMethod]
[DynamicData(nameof(LengthCases))]
public void Validate_LengthEdgeCases(
    string input, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
{
    var result = new InputValidator().Validate(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
}
```

**Benefits:** Adding a new case is just one new `[DataRow(...)]` line — no new method needed. Each data row still shows as a separate test in the runner. Want me to apply this refactor?
